</section>
<section id="A4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.5 </span>Natural vs. Unnatural Neighborhoods Experiment Setup</h3>

<div id="A4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide a detailed explanation of the experimental setup introduced in Sec. <a href="#S4" title="4 Method ‣ A Diffusion Model for Regular Time Series Generation from Irregular Data with Completion and Masking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experiment Setup.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first generate <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> two-dimensional samples <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p\}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from a multivariate Gaussian distribution, with means centered at <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. A figure illustrating all sampled data appears in Figure  <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Irregular time series ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ A Diffusion Model for Regular Time Series Generation from Irregular Data with Completion and Masking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>A. To better simulate our real world environment, we transform each 2D datapoint into a <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 4</annotation></semantics></math> image by setting all pixels to zero except those at the center, which correspond to the <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> coordinates of the original point (e.g., <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s[1,1]=p[0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s[1,1]=p[0]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s[1,2]=p[1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s[1,2]=p[1]</annotation></semantics></math>). Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Irregular time series ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ A Diffusion Model for Regular Time Series Generation from Irregular Data with Completion and Masking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>B depicts an example of this transformation. We refer to this dataset as <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{irregular}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>irregular</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{irregular}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as it simulates an “irregular” dataset containing zeros for missing values.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we construct a second dataset, <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{regular}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>regular</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{regular}}</annotation></semantics></math>, in which the zero entries are replaced with linear or nonlinear transformations of <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p[0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p[0]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p[1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p[1]</annotation></semantics></math>, or both. This is intended to emulate a data-imputation step performed on the fist step of our method, yielding a more “natural” neighborhood of values. An data point example can be found in Figure  <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Irregular time series ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ A Diffusion Model for Regular Time Series Generation from Irregular Data with Completion and Masking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>C.
We then compare three training setups:</p>
<ol id="A4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Train a diffusion model to predict the score across the <em id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">entire</em> image (i.e., noise prediction in diffusion) on <math id="A4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{irregular}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>irregular</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{irregular}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Train a diffusion model to predict <em id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">only</em> the two central (coordinate) pixels, using our masking technique, on <math id="A4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{irregular}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>irregular</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{irregular}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Train the same masked model as in (2), but on <math id="A4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{regular}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>regular</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{regular}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where we have “natural neighbors.”</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">All score models share a simple architecture: a <span id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Conv2D</span> layer with a <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 4</annotation></semantics></math> kernel, followed by a <span id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ReLU</span> activation, and a deconvolution layer that restores the original input size.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ two metrics:</p>
<ol id="A4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Score Estimation Loss:</span> For fair comparison, we measure the score prediction error on the two central pixels (i.e., the original coordinates), regardless of the training strategy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kernel Analysis:</span> We inspect the first-layer convolution kernels to determine which pixels the model focuses on. Since there are <math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math> output channels, we compute the <math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> norm at each spatial position across all channels and then average them.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Additional Experiments and Analysis</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>“Unnatural” Neighborhoods: Stock Market Experiment</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As described in the main paper, we extend the synthetic experiment to a real-world dataset of stock prices. Implementation details appear in the main text; here we present the visual results (moved to the appendix due to space constraints). See Fig. <a href="#A5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ E.1 “Unnatural” Neighborhoods: Stock Market Experiment ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ A Diffusion Model for Regular Time Series Generation from Irregular Data with Completion and Masking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F7" class="ltx_figure"><span id="A5.F7.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:344.0pt;height:51.6pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="456.95" height="68.54" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,68.54) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="translate(0,72.00) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="467.98" height="72.00" overflow="visible"><img src="2510.06699v1/figs/stocks_kernel_exp.png" id="A5.F7.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:contain" width="476" height="75" alt="Refer to caption"></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(226.1,-9.52)"><g transform="scale(1,-1)"><text xml:id="A5.F7.pic1.2">score </text></g><g transform="translate(0,8.50) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="73.19" height="8.50" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="=0.81" text="absent = 0.81" xml:id="A5.F7.pic1.m1">
                <XMath xml:id="A5.F7.pic1.m1.1">
                  <XMApp xml:id="A5.F7.pic1.m1.1.1">
                    <XMTok meaning="equals" role="RELOP">=</XMTok>
                    <XMTok meaning="absent"/>
                    <XMTok meaning="0.81" role="NUMBER">0.81</XMTok>
                  </XMApp>
                </XMath>
              </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(299.88,-9.52)"><g transform="scale(1,-1)"><text xml:id="A5.F7.pic1.3">score </text></g><g transform="translate(0,8.50) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="73.19" height="8.50" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="=0.79" text="absent = 0.79" xml:id="A5.F7.pic1.m2">
                <XMath xml:id="A5.F7.pic1.m2.1">
                  <XMApp xml:id="A5.F7.pic1.m2.1.1">
                    <XMTok meaning="equals" role="RELOP">=</XMTok>
                    <XMTok meaning="absent"/>
                    <XMTok meaning="0.79" role="NUMBER">0.79</XMTok>
                  </XMApp>
                </XMath>
              </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(371.27,-9.52)"><g transform="scale(1,-1)"><text xml:id="A5.F7.pic1.4">score </text></g><g transform="translate(0,8.50) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="70.27" height="8.50" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="=\mathbf{0.29}" text="absent = 0.29" xml:id="A5.F7.pic1.m3">
                <XMath xml:id="A5.F7.pic1.m3.1">
                  <XMApp xml:id="A5.F7.pic1.m3.1.1">
                    <XMTok meaning="equals" role="RELOP">=</XMTok>
                    <XMTok meaning="absent"/>
                    <XMTok font="bold" meaning="0.29" role="NUMBER">0.29</XMTok>
                  </XMApp>
                </XMath>
              </Math></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="A5.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Unnatural vs. natural neighborhoods on stock data. A data point (A) is mapped to an image with zeros and its coordinates centered (B). Denoising the entire image produces inferior kernels (D) compared to masking (E). Constructing natural neighborhoods (C) yields consistent kernels and improved scores (F).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Extending to Categorical and Numerical Features</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further extend our evaluation to demonstrate that our method can handle mixed-type time series containing both numerical and categorical features. Categorical variables are mapped to learnable embedding vectors, which are jointly optimized with the generative model, transforming the input into a fully continuous representation that the diffusion process can operate on seamlessly. During generation, embeddings are mapped back to discrete categories using a simple postprocessing step. To validate this capability, we evaluated our method on the Traffic dataset which includes both numerical and categorical attributes, across multiple sequence lengths and missing rates. As reported in Tab. <a href="#A5.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ E.2 Extending to Categorical and Numerical Features ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ A Diffusion Model for Regular Time Series Generation from Irregular Data with Completion and Masking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, our approach achieves the best discriminative scores compared to prior methods, demonstrating its ability to effectively capture temporal dependencies in mixed-type time series while maintaining superior generative performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T13" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A5.T13.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 13</span>: </span><span id="A5.T13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Discriminative scores on mixed-type data (Traffic) with 30%, 50%, and 70% drop-rate for sequence lengths of <math id="A5.T13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A5.T13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>96</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">96</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
